{"fields":{"advisory":{"aliases":["GHSA-jh2m-fhpj-3vr7"],"categories":[],"collection":"crates","cvss":null,"date":"2026-10-27","description":"The extended-attribute functions in `rustix::fs` (`getxattr`, `lgetxattr`, `fgetxattr`, `listxattr`, `llistxattr` and `flistxattr`) write into a `Buffer` and then mark as many bytes initialized as the system call reported. When the buffer has zero length, the operating system treats the call as a size query: it writes nothing and returns the size of the attribute value or of the name list. rustix still marked that many bytes as initialized.\n\nPassing a `Vec` whose length equals its capacity through `spare_capacity` gives such a zero-length buffer, and rustix then set the vector's length past its capacity over memory that was never written. This is undefined behavior reachable from safe code. With an empty `&mut [MaybeUninit<u8>]` the call panics instead.\n\nFrom 1.1.6 these functions return an empty result for a zero-length buffer, and new `getxattr_query`, `lgetxattr_query`, `fgetxattr_query`, `listxattr_query`, `llistxattr_query` and `flistxattr_query` functions return the size. The 0.38 series did not have the `Buffer` API and is not affected.","expect-deleted":false,"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0486","informational":"unsound","keywords":["xattr","uninitialized-memory"],"license":"CC0-1.0","package":"rustix","references":[],"related":[],"source":null,"title":"xattr functions mark unwritten bytes as initialized when given a zero-length buffer","url":"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/rustix/commit/8e6aaa6713d2bb64b1fdd8c472e3a32a10a76d70","withdrawn":null},"code":"unsound","graphs":[{"Krate":{"name":"rustix","version":"1.1.4"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"agent-client-protocol","version":"2.0.0"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"aionui-ai-agent","version":"0.2.2"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"aionui-app","version":"0.2.2"}},{"Krate":{"name":"aionui-channel","version":"0.2.2"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"aionui-app","version":"0.2.2"},